This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

Hi Roslyn folks! 😊

While chasing a build that hung on my Windows ARM64 dev box, I traced it into `GeneratedNameParser.TryParseFileTypeName`, and wanted to share what I found, along with a cheap way Roslyn could sidestep it.

(*Root cause found with AI assistance, from a dump I captured* 🤖)

----

**Version Used**:

Visual Studio 18 on Windows 11 ARM64: the .NET Framework compiler server, `MSBuild\Current\Bin\Roslyn\VBCSCompiler.exe`, which is an ARM64 build and runs on .NET Framework 4.8.1 ARM64, with `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.dll` 5.11.0 (5.1100.26.42405). The same code is on `main` today.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

It's a race, so it's rare in a real build: we've hit it twice in many local builds. Both times `MSBuild.exe` was compiling a CsWinRT projection project that references `Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET`.

The race itself is in .NET Framework's `Regex`, and it reproduces without Roslyn. This program runs the same pattern and options as `s_fileTypeOrdinalPattern` from every core at once. Build it for ARM64 (`csc -platform:arm64 Program.cs`, or `<PlatformTarget>ARM64</PlatformTarget>`), since an AnyCPU build runs under x64 emulation there:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Threading;

static class Program
{
    // Same pattern and options as GeneratedNameParser.s_fileTypeOrdinalPattern.
    static readonly Regex s_fileTypePattern =
        new Regex(@"<([a-zA-Z_0-9]*)>F([0-9A-F]{64})__", RegexOptions.Compiled);

    static void Main()
    {
        // Mostly ordinary type names (20 to 120 characters, no '<'), plus a file-local one in every 64.
        var random = new Random(1);
        var names = new string[4096];
        for (int i = 0; i < names.Length; i++)
        {
            var name = new StringBuilder();
            int length = 20 + random.Next(101);
            for (int j = 0; j < length; j++) name.Append("ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789_"[random.Next(63)]);
            names[i] = i % 64 == 0 ? "<File" + i + ">F" + new string('A', 64) + "__Type" + i : name.ToString();
        }

        int threadCount = Environment.ProcessorCount;
        var matches = new long[threadCount * 16]; // padded, one slot per cache line
        for (int t = 0; t < threadCount; t++)
        {
            int id = t;
            new Thread(() =>
            {
                for (int i = id; ; i++)
                {
                    s_fileTypePattern.Match(names[i & (names.Length - 1)]);
                    Volatile.Write(ref matches[id * 16], matches[id * 16] + 1);
                }
            }) { IsBackground = true }.Start();
        }

        var seen = new long[threadCount];
        var stalledSeconds = new int[threadCount];
        for (int second = 1; second <= 60; second++)
        {
            Thread.Sleep(1000);
            for (int t = 0; t < threadCount; t++)
            {
                long now = Volatile.Read(ref matches[t * 16]);
                stalledSeconds[t] = now == seen[t] ? stalledSeconds[t] + 1 : 0;
                seen[t] = now;
                if (stalledSeconds[t] == 3)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"Thread {t} has been stuck in Regex.Match for 3 s (at {second} s).");
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine("No hang in 60 s.");
    }
}
```

On a 12-core ARM64 machine it hung in 1 of 3 one-minute runs, after 48 s. A fuller version of the same test, which also prints the runner's state, hung in 2 of 3, after 0.5 s and 14 s. With a `Regex` per thread, that version ran 6.4 billion matches in 2 minutes without hanging, and on .NET 10 a shared one ran 1.2 billion.

**Diagnostic Id**:

N/A, this is a hang.

**Expected Behavior**:

The compilation should finish.

**Actual Behavior**:

`VBCSCompiler.exe` keeps one core busy and the compilation never finishes. In a dump, one thread had used **38 minutes** of CPU here:

```text
System.Text.RegularExpressions.RegexRunner.Scan
System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Run
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Symbols.GeneratedNameParser.TryParseFileTypeName
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Symbols.Metadata.PE.PENamedTypeSymbol..ctor
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Symbols.Metadata.PE.PENamedTypeSymbol.Create
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Symbols.Metadata.PE.PENamespaceSymbol.LazyInitializeTypes
...
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Symbols.SourceNamespaceSymbol.ForceComplete
Roslyn.Utilities.RoslynParallel.For
```

The compilation's own thread was waiting on it in `Parallel.For`. Ending the server lets MSBuild fall back, and the build then finishes.

### Why it happens

The input was an ordinary 81-character name from `WinRT.GenericTypeInstantiations`, `Windows_Foundation_Collections_IVectorView_1_Windows_Devices_Sms_SmsBroadcastType`. It has no `<`, so this isn't backtracking.

- The cached `RegexRunner` had `runtextend` = 98, another input's length, while `Scan` had saved 81 as its stop position. Each failed `FindFirstChar` sets `runtextpos = runtextend`, which never equals 81, so `Scan` bumps it and tries again, forever. A match timeout wouldn't help: `Scan` only checks it after `FindFirstChar` succeeds.
- The runner got there because .NET Framework's `Regex` caches one runner in an `ExclusiveReference`, and on ARM64 its `Release` unlocks with two plain stores:

  ```text
  str xzr,[x19,#0x10]   ; _obj = null
  str wzr,[x19,#0x18]   ; _locked = 0
  ```

  With no `dmb` or `stlr`, the previous user's writes to the runner can land after the next user has taken it. The cache's own fields were clean (`_locked` 1, `_obj` equal to `_ref`), so the 98 came from the thread that had the runner *before*. x64 doesn't reorder stores, so it never shows up there 🙃. Modern .NET's cache is a `volatile` field, which doesn't have the problem.
- Roslyn runs into it often: [`PENamedTypeSymbol`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/d53e65ceaf037efb4f0c43c5a2ae4a3e19f5a59f/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Metadata/PE/PENamedTypeSymbol.cs#L369) calls `TryParseFileTypeName` for every top-level type it loads from metadata, from many binder threads at once, and they all share [one static `Regex`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/d53e65ceaf037efb4f0c43c5a2ae4a3e19f5a59f/src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Symbols/Synthesized/GeneratedNameParser.cs#L210).

### A possible fix in Roslyn

The real fix belongs in .NET Framework's `System.dll`, but since Framework is in maintenance mode, Roslyn could sidestep it. The pattern needs a `<`, so returning early when the name has none keeps the same results and skips nearly every call:

```csharp
if (generatedName.IndexOf(FileTypeNameStartChar) < 0)
{
    checksum = null;
    displayFileName = null;
    originalTypeName = null;
    return false;
}
```

Parsing the name by hand, as `TryParseGeneratedName` does, would avoid `Regex` entirely. On the build side, the .NET-hosted compiler (`RoslynCompilerType=Core` with the .NET 10 SDK) doesn't have the bug.

